WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.520
This is the VOA special English technology report a

00:05.200 --> 00:10.640
Few degrees can make a big difference when it comes to food storage

00:11.320 --> 00:14.400
Foods can go bad if they get too warm

00:15.080 --> 00:17.080
But for many of the world's poor

00:17.520 --> 00:21.440
Finding a good way to keep food cool is difficult

00:22.600 --> 00:25.760
refrigerators are costly and they need electricity

00:26.120 --> 00:32.880
Yet spoiled food not only creates health risks, but also

00:33.560 --> 00:35.280
economic losses

00:35.280 --> 00:41.360
Farmers lose money when they have to throw away products that they cannot sell quickly

00:42.440 --> 00:43.760
but in

00:43.760 --> 00:50.920
1995 a teacher in northern Nigeria named Mohammed Bahaba found a solution

00:51.600 --> 00:54.320
He developed the pot-in-pot

00:55.400 --> 00:57.400
conservation cooling system

00:58.200 --> 01:06.560
It uses two round containers made of clay a smaller pot is placed inside a larger one

01:07.160 --> 01:11.320
The space between the two pots is filled with wet sand

01:11.920 --> 01:17.720
The inner pot can be filled with fruit vegetables or drinks a

01:18.320 --> 01:22.180
Wet cloth covers the whole cooling system

01:23.120 --> 01:29.280
Food stored in the smaller pot is kept from spoiling through a simple

01:30.160 --> 01:32.040
evaporation process

01:32.040 --> 01:35.000
water in the sand between the two pots

01:36.040 --> 01:43.240
evaporates through the surface of the larger pot where drier outside air is moving

01:44.040 --> 01:50.640
The evaporation process creates a drop in temperature of several degrees

01:51.560 --> 01:57.800
This cools the inner pot and helps keep food safe from harmful bacteria

01:58.840 --> 02:02.880
Some foods can be kept fresh this way for several weeks

02:04.000 --> 02:06.000
people throughout Nigeria

02:06.240 --> 02:08.740
began using the invention and

02:09.460 --> 02:14.580
It became popular with farmers in other African countries

02:15.740 --> 02:17.740
Mohammed Bahaba personally

02:18.460 --> 02:20.460
Financed the first

02:20.540 --> 02:26.340
5000 pot-in-pot systems for his own community and five villages

02:26.740 --> 02:28.740
nearby in

02:29.020 --> 02:32.620
2000 the Rolex watch company of Switzerland

02:33.220 --> 02:36.900
Honored him with the Rolex Award for Enterprise

02:37.900 --> 02:45.580
This award recognizes people trying to develop projects aimed at improving human knowledge and

02:46.620 --> 02:48.380
well-being a

02:48.380 --> 02:52.260
committee considers projects in science and medicine

02:53.460 --> 02:55.140
technology

02:55.140 --> 02:56.740
exploration and

02:56.740 --> 02:59.140
discovery the environment and

03:00.340 --> 03:02.340
cultural history

03:02.980 --> 03:09.580
Winners receive a financial assistance to help develop and extend their projects

03:10.340 --> 03:17.340
The awards are given every two years. The next awards will be presented in late

03:18.300 --> 03:20.300
2012

03:21.060 --> 03:22.780
Last year

03:22.780 --> 03:28.380
Rolex launched the Young Laureates program for the younger generation

03:28.940 --> 03:34.140
For VOA special English, I'm Carolyn Pursuti

03:34.940 --> 03:37.020
We have more programs at

03:38.100 --> 03:40.100
VOA special English

03:40.380 --> 03:41.540
calm

03:41.540 --> 03:43.540
where people can read

03:43.700 --> 03:47.220
listen and learn English with texts

03:48.060 --> 03:49.900
MP3's and

03:49.900 --> 03:51.460
activities

03:51.460 --> 03:57.500
We also offer English lessons at the VOA learning English page on

